<title>BELGIUM: EU warns it may suspend cereal product licences.</title>
<headline>EU warns it may suspend cereal product licences.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-01-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Commission has warned it may use its GATT (General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade) powers to suspend cereals-based processed products export licences, an EU official said.</p>
<p>&quot;The Commission is concerned about heavy licence booking and the possibility of speculation,&quot; the official said.</p>
<p>The Commission told the European Union's cereals management committee on Thursday it was concerned about the situation because refunds were high.</p>
<p>It suggested that some traders might be booking licences ahead of contracts and asked national authorities to monitor applications carefully.</p>
<p>Refunds were not cut because, the official said, the rising value of the dollar and higher world prices were reducing the cost of refunds.</p>
<p>But the Commission warned that it might have to use its so-called GATT period of reflection, which allows it to suspend licence applications for three days, the official said.</p>
<p>Currently basic rate refunds (which are adjusted by a coefficient) are:</p>
<p> maize	    39.00   Ecus /t</p>
<p> wheat	    12.59   Ecus /t</p>
<p> durum wheat     7.59   Ecus /t</p>
<p> barley	   31.76   Ecus /t</p>
<p> oats	     29.22   Ecus /t</p>
<p> rye		33.09   Ecus /t</p>
<p> glucose	  32.04   Ecus /t</p>
<p> potato starch  39.00   Ecus /t</p>
<p>-- Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6800</p>
</text>
